A||N||6||Will||Camber gain vs. Roll center height||||1||
Z||000000||Will||12-31-2000||11:21 AM||||I have come up with a configuration to convert '88 rear suspension to short/long A-arms. This setup has outstanding camber characteristics (with -0.5 static camber, the outside camber never goes positive, even with 5 degrees of body roll), and excellent roll center movement characteristics (moves less than 4 inches side to side at 5 degrees of body roll), but the roll center is just a little high (3.2" static, 3.5" at 5 degrees body roll). I can move the roll center down, but I sacrifice camber gain and side to side stability for the roll center.<BR>     What's more important--camber gain or roll center height?<BR>     Experiences, anecdotal and otherwise, welcome as well as technical discussion.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||12-31-2000||01:27 PM||||I was racing an unprepped sixties model ford(this was IN the sixties) and had a front tire go so positive the bead seal failed with drastic results.  Now I am a big fan of keeping the wheel assembly perpendicular to the road.<BR>That is not a problem the Fiero will ever experience, but I expect low profile tires are even more sensitive to camber changes than the old bias ply tires ever were.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Wannabe||12-31-2000||04:10 PM||||In my limited (for now) experience with the Fiero on a race track, I found that even the earlier ('87 GT) stock rear suspension did a decent job of keeping the tire flat. With 225-55-15 tires, it produced a nice even wear pattern across the face of the tire. The most important aspect really is niether the roll center height or camber gain (within limits of course) but the change in the location of the roll center with suspension movement. If that's as good as you can get it, you're on the right track. The only other concern will be straight line traction. If the camber gain  is happens mostly near the end of suspension travel, then that should be OK too. Oh, also, be mindful of the difference of roll center fron to back. Sounds neat.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||12-31-2000||04:25 PM||||The '88 trailing arm acts like the lower half of a 4-link. I haven't designed that yet, but it should be pretty easy to get good anti-squat out of that by tilting the UCA down in front a little. I may also want to raise the forward pivot of the trailing arm an inch or so.<P>I will be doing a similar design for '84-'87 rear when I have the chance to take one apart and do some measurements on the hub carrier.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||01-01-2001||07:38 PM||||Assuming perfect camber, how high is practical for the roll center to be? At what RC height does the car start to lift the inside wheel on corners?<BR>Does anyone have examples of high and low roll center cars which I could go test drive?<P>High high is the Fiero's rear roll center? My suspension program doesn't do struts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||01-03-2001||10:55 PM||||Will,you can use the strut angle + 90 degrees for the upper control arm.<BR>I just got my modified '87 to do nice,controlled 4-wheel drifts at higher speeds with a recent spring change,finally,I'm happy with the suspension.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||01-04-2001||12:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>Will,you can use the strut angle + 90 degrees for the upper control arm.<BR>I just got my modified '87 to do nice,controlled 4-wheel drifts at higher speeds with a recent spring change,finally,I'm happy with the suspension.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What suspension mods do you have besides springs?||||reg||1
